75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (In Afghanistan in 2011 and 2012, a drone, to me, meant an MQ-9 Reaper. I had never seen one in person, much less fought with one overhead. By 2023, when I volunteered as a training advisor in Zaporizhzhia, teaching NATO fire-support procedures to Ukrainian military and territorial defense units, drones ran through nearly everything I taught: how to hide from one, how to call for fire using one, and how to operate with one watching your every move. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
76 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Thirteen years separated that demonstration in Korea from my time in Ukraine. I empathize deeply with the horse cavalryman who served long enough to see the tank. That experience informs the questions I bring to CSIS: what changes for the people on the ground, and how quickly can our doctrine, training, and policy catch up?)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
77 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 50 0 R
/T (How do you assess the current drone threat landscape, and what challenges are driving today's counter-UAS priorities? )
/E (How do you assess the current drone threat landscape, and what challenges are driving today's counter-UAS priorities? )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
78 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Much of the excellent work my peers in the UAS field produce focuses on one-way attack drones and swarms. The challenge that most concerns me is persistent battlefield observation: drones have made it much harder to move, resupply, or concentrate forces without being seen. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
79 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (In Afghanistan, being outside the wire certainly carried its own stresses, but I remember long stretches when I felt reasonably safe despite being deployed to a war zone.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
