/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (speed. What concerns me is whether we retain the capability, relationships, and trained personnel assembled for it. A major event ending safely should reinforce the case for preparedness; it can also make that preparedness easier to take for granted. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (The alleged plot against the UFC Freedom 250 event at the White House in June illustrates the problem. According to the Justice Department, the conspirators planned to use explosive-armed drones to force an evacuation, then have snipers fire on targets within the fleeing crowd. Their messages discussed raising about $1,300 for drones and explosive charges. That was their proposed budget, but it shows how little money they believed they needed to add drones to a coordinated attack.  )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2 184 0 R 4]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [3]
/Pg 13 0 R
/P 184 0 R
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (The lesson I take is that drone threats belong in routine security planning for mass gatherings and critical infrastructure, including how an aerial threat changes the response on the ground. That planning cannot depend on an event being prominent enough to warrant a federal task force.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (A second lesson is the changing direction of expertise. This spring, Ukraine deployed interceptor drones and specialists to help defend U.S. bases in Jordan following an American request. I trained Ukrainian units on NATO fire-support procedures in 2023; three years later, they are supplying us with operators and techniques. Interoperability now has to account for partners whose combat experience in the drone domain exceeds our own.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6 185 0 R 8]
>>
endobj
